{"kind":"stack","id":"stk-materials","title":"Everyday materials: choosing the right material","theme":{"background":"#f4f7ee","foreground":"#1f2d1a","font":"Georgia, serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-t","content":"Fair test"},{"type":"text","id":"c1-e","content":"A test where only one thing is changed at a time, so we know what caused the result."},{"type":"button","id":"c1-b","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-q","content":"Which material would be best for a saucepan, so the heat from the cooker spreads through it quickly?"},{"type":"button","id":"c2-a","label":"Metal","why":"Metals conduct heat well, so the heat spreads through the pan quickly.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c2-b","label":"Wood","why":"Pupils may think wood is strong, but wood is a poor conductor of heat.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c2-c","label":"Plastic","why":"Pupils may think plastic is tough, but plastic is also a poor conductor of heat.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"Why are wooden spoons used for stirring hot soup?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"Wood does not get very hot to touch","why":"Wood is a poor conductor, so the handle stays cool while the spoon is in hot soup.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"Wood is the heaviest material","why":"Pupils may think heavy things are safer, but weight is not why wood is chosen.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"Wood is the shiniest material","why":"Pupils may link shininess with cooking, but shine does not affect heat transfer.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-s","content":"You are designing a raincoat for a school trip. Which choice do you make?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"A plastic coat, which keeps water out","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"A thin cotton coat, which soaks up water","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-s","content":"Plastic is waterproof, so the rain runs off and you stay dry. Well done!"},{"type":"button","id":"c5-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-s","content":"Cotton soaks up the rain and you get cold and wet. Next time, test materials first!"},{"type":"button","id":"c6-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-t","content":"Conductor"},{"type":"text","id":"c7-e","content":"A material that lets heat or electricity pass through it easily, such as metal."},{"type":"button","id":"c7-b","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-q","content":"Why are electrical wires covered in plastic?"},{"type":"button","id":"c8-a","label":"Plastic does not let electricity pass through it easily","why":"Plastic is an insulator, so the electricity stays inside the wire and the person stays safe.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-b","label":"Plastic makes electricity flow faster","why":"Pupils may think plastic speeds things up, but plastic does not make current faster.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-c","label":"Plastic is a good conductor of electricity","why":"Pupils may mix up plastic with metal, but plastic is a poor conductor.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-q","content":"Which material is best for a bridge that must hold heavy loads?"},{"type":"button","id":"c9-a","label":"Steel","why":"Steel is strong and hard, so it can hold heavy loads without bending or breaking.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"Paper","why":"Pupils may think paper is strong, but paper tears and bends easily under weight.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-c","label":"Rubber","why":"Pupils may think rubber is tough, but rubber stretches and bends too much under heavy loads.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"Why is wood used to make a garden fence?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"Wood is strong and lasts well outdoors","why":"Wood is strong and can be treated to last outside, so it suits a garden fence.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"Wood melts in the rain","why":"Pupils may think wood melts, but wood does not melt in rain; it can rot if untreated.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"Wood is see-through","why":"Pupils may confuse wood with glass, but wood is not transparent.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-q","content":"Which material would make the best cover for a cold drink can to keep the drink cold?"},{"type":"button","id":"c11-a","label":"Plastic","why":"Plastic is a poor conductor, so it slows the heat from outside reaching the drink.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c12"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c11-b","label":"Aluminium foil","why":"Pupils may think foil keeps things cold, but metal conducts heat in quickly.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c12"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c11-c","label":"Steel","why":"Pupils may think steel is safe, but steel conducts heat well too.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c12"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c12","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c12-h","content":"Well done: you finished the materials lesson!"},{"type":"text","id":"c12-m","content":"You now know how to choose materials using evidence from fair tests. Keep testing and asking why."}],"actions":[]}}
